%% 21.8 Summary
%% ============================================================
% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Instantiating an Open Row at a Closed Record}

A function may read named fields while leaving the rest of a record
polymorphic. For one instantiation of such a function, compare the
required fields and bind its row variable to the exact remainder.
Assume unique labels, finite maps for rows, concrete field types with
decidable structural equality, and a fresh flexible tail variable
$r$. Input $P$ is the mandatory part of the parameter row
$\{P\mid r\}$; input $A$ is a closed actual row. There is no
subtyping or implicit coercion. The routine either gives the
substitution for $r$ or reports the first incompatible required field.

\begin{sudocode}[Match a required row prefix]
\Function{MatchRow}{$P,r,A$}
  \State $B\gets$ copy of $A$ \Comment{Candidate tail}
  \ForAll{$(\ell,T)$ in $P$}
    \If{$\ell\notin\operatorname{dom}(B)$}
      \State \Return missing field $\ell$
    \EndIf
    \If{$B[\ell]\ne T$}
      \State \Return type mismatch at $\ell$
    \EndIf
    \State Remove $\ell$ from $B$ \Comment{Forbid duplicate labels}
  \EndFor
  \State \Return substitution $r\mapsto B$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
A function reads \texttt{name} as \texttt{String} and
\texttt{active} as \texttt{Bool}. At a call with fields
\texttt{name:String}, \texttt{active:Bool}, and
\texttt{score:Int}, the initial remainder has all three.
Removing \texttt{name} leaves \texttt{active} and
\texttt{score}; removing \texttt{active} leaves only
\texttt{score:Int}. That singleton is the instantiation of $r$.
If the function returns the original row, substituting it recovers
a result type where the caller can still read \texttt{score}.
A second call with \texttt{active:Int} fails at the type comparison,
even though the label is present.

\Cref{fig:visual-21} shows the partition of fields that an open
row preserves at this call site.

