\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
After consuming some required fields, every consumed field has
matched the actual type and $B$ contains exactly the fields not
yet consumed. The resulting row is unique because labels cannot
repeat, and the remainder can be empty. A new call must instantiate
the quantified variable afresh, rather than retain the previous
tail. This procedure does not solve general row equations with
open rows on both sides or with shared tail variables elsewhere
in the type.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:polymorphism-summary}

Polymorphism is one of the most important abstractions in programming
language design.  The three forms---parametric, ad hoc, and subtype---serve
different purposes and have different implementation strategies.

Parametric polymorphism (System~F) provides uniform behavior across types,
with strong guarantees (parametricity, free theorems).  Type inference for
parametric polymorphism (Hindley--Milner) is a landmark achievement that
makes polymorphism practical without type annotations.

Ad hoc polymorphism (type classes) provides type-specific behavior with a
uniform interface.  The dictionary translation makes it efficient and
composable.

Subtype polymorphism provides flexible, extensible hierarchies but
introduces complexity (variance, decidability issues).  Bounded
quantification (System~F$_{<:}$) combines parametric and subtype
polymorphism.

Row polymorphism provides a lightweight form of structural subtyping for
records and variants, enabling flexible, extensible data structures.

Each form of polymorphism has its place, and modern languages typically
combine several forms to provide maximum expressiveness.
